Instruction:
Write a C++ function that takes a reference to a std::string containing markdown formatted text and returns a std::string containing html formatted text.

Response:

|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\| Here's a C++ function that converts markdown text to HTML:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <map>
#include <regex>

// Define a map to map markdown syntax to HTML tags
std::map<std::string, std::string> markdownToHtml = {
    {"*bold*", "<b>$1</b>"},
    {"*italic*", "<i>$1</i>"},
    {"_italic_", "<i>$1</i>"},
    {"**bold**", "<b>$1</b>"},
    {"~~strikethrough~~", "<s>$1</s>"},
    {"[link](http://example.com) =", "<a href=\"http://example.com\">$1</a>"},
    {"[link](http://example.com) <i>text</i>", "<a href=\"http://example.com\">$1</i></a>"}
};

/**
 * Converts markdown text to HTML.
 *
 * @param markdown The markdown text to convert.
 * @return The HTML text.
 */
std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html = markdown;
    for (const auto& pair : markdownToHtml) {
        std::regex pattern(pair.first);
        html = std::regex_replace(html, pattern, pair.second);
    }
    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "*bold* _italic_ **bold** ~~strikethrough~~ [link](http://example.com) <i>text</i>";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a map to map markdown syntax to HTML tags. It then uses the `std::regex_replace` function to replace all occurrences of markdown syntax with the corresponding HTML tags. The function returns the resulting HTML text.

Note that this implementation only supports a limited subset of markdown syntax. For a more comprehensive implementation, you may need to add more entries to the `markdownToHtml` map and use more advanced regular expressions.

